**Migraine** is a common condition that causes attacks of **moderate to severe headache**, often with other symptoms. It affects around **1 in 5 women** and **1 in 15 men**. Attacks can be very disabling, and some people have them several times a month.

## Symptoms

A migraine attack often includes:

- a **throbbing headache**, often on one side of the head
- **feeling or being sick**
- being **sensitive to light, sound or smells**
- pain that gets **worse when you move**

An attack usually lasts from **4 hours to 3 days**. Afterwards, many people feel drained for a day or so.

### Aura

About 1 in 3 people with migraine have an **aura**, a warning sign that usually comes before the headache. It can include:

- **zigzag lines**, flashing lights or blind spots in your vision
- **pins and needles** or numbness, often starting in one hand
- **difficulty speaking**

An aura usually lasts less than an hour.

## Triggers

Attacks do not always have a clear cause, but common triggers include:

- **hormone changes**, such as around a period
- **stress**, or relaxing after stress
- **tiredness** or changes to your sleep
- **skipping meals** or not drinking enough water
- **alcohol** and some foods
- **bright or flickering lights**

A **headache diary** can help you and your GP spot your triggers and patterns.

## Diagnosis

There is no test for migraine. A GP will usually diagnose it from your symptoms and an examination. Keep a diary for a few weeks before your appointment if you can.

{% callout type="danger" title="Call 999 or go to A&E if you have" %}
- a **sudden, very severe headache**, the worst you have ever had, that comes on in seconds or minutes
- a headache with a **stiff neck, fever or a rash**
- a headache with **weakness, numbness, slurred speech, confusion** or a drooping face
- a headache after a **head injury**
{% /callout %}

## Treatment

There are medicines to **stop an attack** and medicines to **prevent attacks**. See [migraine treatment](/conditions/brain-nerves-and-senses/migraine/treatment/).
